# Regression protection before a commit is allowed to land.
#
# This hook previously ran lint-staged alone. lint-staged formats and
# lints the staged files, which catches style but says nothing about
# whether the code still works — so a commit that broke a test, or that
# did not typecheck, was accepted without complaint. That is not
# hypothetical: nav.test.ts was broken and committed in exactly this way,
# and the break was only noticed later.
#
# Four gates, cheapest first, so an obvious mistake fails in seconds
# rather than after the slowest step.

# Without this, a failing gate in the middle would print its error and
# the hook would still exit on the status of the last command — the
# quietest possible way to lose the whole point of the hook.
set -e

# 1 + 2. Format and lint the staged files, then run the tests that
#        import them (`vitest related`, configured in lint-staged).
#
# lint-staged stashes unstaged work first, so these run against exactly
# what is being committed rather than what happens to be in the tree.
bun x lint-staged

# 3. Types across the whole project.
#
# Not restricted to staged files on purpose: changing an exported type
# breaks its consumers, and those are the files the commit does *not*
# touch. ~10s.
bun run typecheck

# 4. The architectural guard tests.
#
# Always, whatever was staged. These read source rather than importing
# it, so `vitest related` cannot see them and reports "No test files
# found, exiting with code 0" for a change to MissionControl.tsx. See
# scripts/contract-tests.mjs. ~10s.
bun run test:contracts

# Deliberately NOT here: the full unit suite. It takes ~48s and includes
# ownership-graph-perf, whose 1000ms wall-clock budget fails only under
# full-suite CPU contention and passes in isolation. A hook that blocks
# commits at random is a hook everyone learns to pass --no-verify to,
# which costs more protection than the suite would add. CI runs it in
# full, on a quiet machine, where a real failure is meaningful.
